 in the 15th century when German inventor Johannes Gutenberg developed the movable type printing press. This invention revolutionized the way information was disseminated, making it possible to produce books and other materials more efficiently and at a lower cost. In the 19th century, the printing industry saw significant advancements. The first rotary press was invented by Robert Hoe in 1843, which allowed for continuous printing on a roll of paper, greatly increasing the speed and efficiency of the printing process. In 1863, the first rotary press was introduced, and in 1865, the first web-fed rotary press was developed by William Bullock. These innovations were crucial in the growth of the newspaper industry, as they enabled the production of larger quantities of printed material in a shorter amount of time. The 19th century also saw the development of the linotype machine, which allowed for the rapid composition of text, further enhancing the efficiency of the printing process. In the 20th century, the printing industry continued to evolve with the introduction of new technologies. In 1904, the first offset printing press was invented by Ira Washington Rubel, which used a rubber blanket to transfer the image from the plate to the paper,
